 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ per pixel, where $N$ is the number of steps in the shadow ray traversal. The shadow ray traversal is the dominant cost when shadows are enabled [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary memory per pixel, as it only requires storing the current position and distance during shadow ray traversal [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** The method is computationally efficient for simple scenes but can become expensive in complex geometries due to the high number of shadow ray samples required for accurate occlusion detection [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Provides clear visual cues for depth and shape, significantly improving the perception of surface details compared to unshadowed shading [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Prone to self-shadowing artifacts if the shadow ray origin is not properly offset [S1, unofficial]. Does not account for indirect lighting or specular reflections [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Suitable for real-time or interactive visualization of isosurfaces where depth perception is critical and computational resources are limited [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** Not suitable for physically accurate global illumination or scenes requiring specular highlights [S1, unofficial].
